+# Module index sandbox benchmark
+
+The sandbox benchmark validates the core indexing algorithm at NixOS-scale cardinality. The sandbox used for development did not contain Rust/Cargo/Nix/GTK system libraries, so the recorded timings are **Python surrogate timings**, not Rust performance claims and not an authoritative measurement of current nixpkgs renderer coverage.
+
+Synthetic corpus:
+
+- 24,000 option records;
+- 2.73 MiB compact JSON;
+- 2,615 inferred `.enable` module roots;
+- nested concrete child modules;
+- dynamic `<name>` paths;
+- read-only fields;
+- scalar, list, attribute-set, enum, submodule, and unknown types.
+
+Measured in the sandbox over repeated timing runs:
+
+| Measurement | Result |
+| --- | ---: |
+| JSON parse median | 22.76 ms |
+| catalog compile median | 55.92 ms |
+| catalog compile p95 | 125.96 ms |
+| 500 indexed lookups | 0.12 ms |
+| peak traced memory (single parse+compile) | 14.16 MiB |
+| deterministic output | yes |
+
+A separate 100,000-option / 10,000-module stress corpus compiled in **276.41 ms median** in the same Python surrogate (11.68 MiB compact JSON).
+
+The important algorithmic change made during the benchmark was replacing an O(options × modules) root scan with O(option path depth) prefix probing.
+
+After the benchmark, renderer policy was made **more conservative** for package/path collections, so the renderer-count fields in the retained JSON benchmark artifacts should be treated as historical first-slice evidence rather than current coverage numbers. The authoritative current coverage must come from `nsc-module-index` against a real current NixOS `options.json` on the target host.
